{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And every meal-offering of yours shall you season with salt.</b> So as to enthrone the Holy One, blessed be He, over all the opposites visible in the world — opposites that led many to depart into heresy, saying: from one beginning two opposites could not have emerged. Now salt has in its nature a thing and its opposite, for it possesses the power of fire and heat and the productions of water, so much so that the masters of Kabbalah said that it corresponds to the attribute of judgment and the attribute of mercy. Therefore it is called 'the covenant of your Elohim,' for through this offering a covenant is cut with HaShem to establish His rule over all the opposites. And all the meal-offerings except that of the priest were eaten by the priests, and this is like charity, which is compared to salt that preserves and sustains meat — so too 'salt of money is lacking' (Ketuvot 66b), and the charity within the offering is greater than the offering itself, as it is written (Proverbs 21:3), ״עֲשֹׂה צְדָקָה וּמִשְׁפָּט נִבְחָר לַה׳ מִזָּבַח״ — 'To do righteousness and justice is chosen by HaShem more than sacrifice.' And concerning this it says, <b>upon every offering of yours you shall offer salt</b> — that the salt ascends over all the offerings and is chosen by HaShem more than sacrifice. And according to its plain sense, <b>upon every offering of yours</b> refers also to the flesh."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The phrase 'salt of money is lacking' (מלח ממון חסר) is a paraphrase of the Talmudic dictum in Ketuvot 66b; the Kli Yakar cites it in compressed form. The word מינות refers to heresy or sectarian departure from faith."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:02.972141221Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}